        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mehmed II</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1432 CE – 1481 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">30 March 1432 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, then the Ottoman capital; son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> and of Hüma Hatun, a slave of uncertain origin</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">3 May 1481, aged forty-nine, after falling ill at Maltepe at the start of a new campaign; the historian Colin Heywood sees substantial circumstantial evidence of poisoning, possibly at the behest of his son Bayezid; buried in his türbe near the Fatih Mosque</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ottoman sultan from August 1444 to September 1446 and from February 1451 to May 1481; governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> as a boy and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> from 1446 to 1451; after 1453 claimed the title caesar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> (Qayser-i Rûm)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> in 1453; the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and of its last states in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>; the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a>; rebuilding and repopulating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>; codifying law in the kanunname; legalising royal fratricide; his library and patronage of Italian artists</span></div>
