    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Made sultan at twelve and set aside two years later, Mehmed returned to the throne in 1451 and at twenty-one took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> after a fifty-seven-day siege, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>.</li><li>His thirty years of war added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and the Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beyliks</a> to the empire, at the cost of mass captivity, executions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a>, and he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> a thriving capital again.</li><li>He centralised government under officials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> background, codified law and legalised the killing of a new sultan's brothers, and he is remembered as a hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and, in the Christian world of his day, as a bloodthirsty tyrant.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>To get past the boom chain across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a> in 1453, Mehmed had about eighty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> carried overland around the Genoese colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a> on a road of wood little over a mile long.</p></aside>
    </section>

