    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1432</span><span class="rf-text">Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> and Hüma Hatun</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1444</span><span class="rf-text">Made sultan at twelve when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> abdicates; Murad returns to command the army and wins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1453</span><span class="rf-text">Takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> after a fifty-seven-day siege and moves the Ottoman capital there</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1456</span><span class="rf-text">Fails to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> and is wounded fighting in person</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1461</span><span class="rf-text">Takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, absorbing the last vestige of Byzantine rule after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1473</span><span class="rf-text">Defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli">Battle of Otlukbeli</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1479</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of Constantinople ends the long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which cedes Shkodra and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1481</span><span class="rf-text">Falls ill at Maltepe at the start of a new campaign and dies</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mehmed was born on 30 March 1432 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, then the Ottoman capital, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> and of Hüma Hatun, a slave of uncertain origin. At eleven he was sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> with two advisers, as Ottoman custom required, and his father sent teachers with him. His Islamic education shaped his outlook; he followed the approach of his mentor Molla Gürâni, and the sheikh Akshamsaddin impressed on him from a young age the duty of conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He also showed an early interest in Classical history and European art: his school books carry sketches of ancient coins and portraits in European style, and he reportedly had tutors in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> who read him Laertius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>.</p><p>After making peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 12 June 1444, Murad abdicated in favour of his twelve-year-old son. When a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> broke the truce that September, Murad led the Ottoman army to victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> on 10 November 1444. Chronicles of the 17th century have Mehmed summoning his father with the words "If you are the sultan, come and lead your armies", but Halil Inalcik holds that it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candarl-halil-pasha-the-younger">Çandarlı Halil Pasha</a> who brought Murad back. In 1446 Murad returned to the throne, and Mehmed, keeping the title of sultan, served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> until his father's death in 1451.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mehmed II&#8221; cites where it covers Early life and first reign, Consorts:</p><ul><li>David Nicolle, Constantinople 1453: The End of Byzantium (Osprey Publishing, 2000)</li><li>John Freely, The Grand Turk: Sultan Mehmet II – Conqueror of Constantinople, Master of an Empire and Lord of Two Seas (I.B. Tauris, 2009)</li><li>Franz Babinger, Mehmed the Conqueror and His Time (Princeton University Press, 1978)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II<span>Father</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On becoming sultan again in 1451, Mehmed first campaigned against İbrahim II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>, who had stirred revolts, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> threatened to release the Ottoman claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orhan">Orhan</a>. He then strengthened the navy and prepared to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Facing the fortress his great-grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> had built on the Asian shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosphorus">Bosphorus</a>, he built Rumelihisarı on the European side, took control of the strait and levied a toll on passing ships; a Venetian vessel that ignored the signal to stop was sunk, its surviving sailors beheaded and its captain impaled as a warning.</p><p>In early April 1453 he laid siege to the city with an army of between 80,000 and 200,000 men, an artillery train of over seventy large pieces, including the great bombard designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orban">Orban</a>, and a fleet of 320 vessels. The walls held at first, and a boom chain closed the harbour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>, so on 22 April he had about eighty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> dragged overland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Galata</a> into the Golden Horn, stretching the defenders along a longer line of walls. Constantinople fell on 29 May after a fifty-seven-day siege. Mehmed, then twenty-one, rode to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, ordered it protected and had it made a mosque, and he moved the Ottoman capital from Adrianople to the conquered city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the conquest Mehmed claimed the title caesar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a claim the scholar George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> supported and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> accepted but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> did not. Year after year he campaigned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> fell in stages from 1454 to the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> on 20 June 1459. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> fell in 1460, its capital Mistra on 29 May, and Trebizond surrendered on 15 August 1461. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> was conquered in 1463, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> taken in 1466, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> defeated at Otlukbeli in 1473. In 1475 his forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> and the Genoese towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and made the Crimean khans tributary princes, and a long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1463 brought him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> in 1470.</p><p>At home he rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>'s walls and built a citadel, a hospital, barracks, a gun foundry, mosques including the Fatih Mosque with its eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapı Palace</a>. He ordered Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to resettle the city, and prisoners of war and deported people were sent there from across the empire; a survey of 1478 counted 16,324 households and an estimated 80,000 people. He restored the Orthodox Patriarchate on 6 January 1454 under Gennadius Scholarius and appointed a grand rabbi. He centralised government under officials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devshirme">devshirme</a> background, made the religious scholars salaried officials, and between 1477 and 1481 issued a kanunname setting out the duties and salaries of the chief officials.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mehmed II&#8221; cites where it covers Early life and first reign, Conquest of Constantinople, Conquest of Serbia (1454–1459), Ottoman-Venetian War (1463–1479):</p><ul><li>David Nicolle, Constantinople 1453: The End of Byzantium (Osprey Publishing, 2000)</li><li>Théoharis Stavrides, The Sultan of Vezirs: The Life and Times of the Ottoman Grand Vezir Mahmud Pasha Angelovic (1453–1474) (Brill, 2001)</li><li>Caroline Finkel, Osman's Dream: The Story of the Ottoman Empire, 1300–1923 (Basic Books, 2007)</li><li>Stanford J Shaw, History of the Ottoman Empire and Modern Turkey (Cambridge University Press, 1976)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mehmed's wars met hard resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> drove him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1456, where he was wounded after personally killing three enemy soldiers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlad-the-impaler">Vlad III Dracula</a> of Wallachia refused tribute in 1459, had the Ottoman envoys killed by nailing their turbans to their heads and impaled the force of Hamza Pasha; in 1462 Vlad nearly reached Mehmed in a night attack before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Târgoviște and set up his brother Radu. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> against him until his death in 1468. In 1475 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> of Moldavia destroyed an Ottoman army at Vaslui, with Ottoman losses beyond 40,000 according to Venetian and Polish records.</p><p>The conquests were violent. The Serbian campaign of 1454 took fifty thousand prisoners, and the captured Serbian voivode Nikola Skobaljić was impaled; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> Mehmed executed King Stephen Tomašević and his uncle Radivoj; his commander Turahanoğlu Ömer Bey laid 2,000 Wallachian heads at his feet. He formally legalised fratricide, declaring that whichever son took the throne might kill his brothers "for the common benefit of the people", and later sultans executed their brothers and all their male descendants. Franz Babinger writes that the Christian world and part of his subjects regarded him as a bloodthirsty tyrant. His interest in Christian art and relics worried traditionalists, and his son Bayezid accused him of "not believing in Muhammad", though Gülru Necipoğlu notes that sources in Islamic languages do not support such a view of his irreligion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In his last years Mehmed pressed the war in the west. He personally led the siege of Shkodra in 1478–79, and under the Treaty of Constantinople of 1479 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> ceded Shkodra, territory on the Dalmatian coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, paid an indemnity of 100,000 ducats and agreed to a yearly tribute of around 10,000 ducats for trading rights in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. In 1480 an army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedik-ahmed-pasha">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otranto">Otranto</a>, leaving a garrison of 800 infantry and 500 cavalry; so soon after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, there was fear that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> would suffer the same fate, and plans were made to evacuate the Pope and the citizens.</p><p>In 1481 Mehmed marched out with the army, planning campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and southern Italy, though some historians think his goal was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. On reaching Maltepe he fell ill, and he died some days later, on 3 May 1481, aged forty-nine. Colin Heywood finds substantial circumstantial evidence that he was poisoned, possibly at the behest of his eldest son and successor Bayezid. He was buried in his türbe near the Fatih Mosque. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> rejoiced: church bells were rung, and Venice proclaimed "The Great Eagle is dead!" Quarrels over the succession, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii">Bayezid II</a> fought his half-brother Cem, possibly kept reinforcements from Otranto, which was retaken in 1481. Bayezid sold his father's art collection.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mehmed's thirty-year reign expanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the Turkish states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, and Constantinople remained the Turkish capital until the 1920s; fifty years later it was again the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The historian Neşri wrote that "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mehmed created all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>". He is recognised as the first sultan to codify criminal and constitutional law, and so established the classical image of the autocratic Ottoman sultan. Older scholarship credited him with creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet-system">millet system</a>, a claim more recent scholarship considers exaggerated, and the story that he founded the Armenian Patriarchate is a legend.</p><p>He gathered Italian artists, humanists and Greek scholars at his court, called Gentile Bellini from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to paint his portrait, and built a library of over 8,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in Persian, Ottoman Turkish, Arabic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek; he also wrote poetry under the name Avni. Historians read this patronage as diplomacy with Italian states and as a claim to legitimacy, presenting him as a new Alexander. He left an imposing reputation in both the Islamic and the Christian worlds. In modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and parts of the Muslim world he is considered a hero: Istanbul's Fatih district, the Fatih Mosque and the Fatih Sultan Mehmet Bridge bear his name, and his picture was on the 1,000 lira note from 1986 to 1992. Rossini made him the subject of the opera Maometto II in 1820.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mehmed II took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and spent three decades extending Ottoman rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, with mass captivity, executions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> along the way. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> as a thriving capital, centralised the state and codified its law, and he is remembered both as a national hero and as a tyrant.</em></p>

